{
  "analysis": "We need to compute, for a given graph where the first N-1 edges form a spanning tree, the sum over all weight permutations (1..M) that make this tree a minimum spanning tree (MST) of the total weight of the MST (which equals the sum of the weights on the tree edges, since the tree is the MST). A permutation is valid (the tree is the MST) iff for every non-tree edge e, its weight is strictly larger than the maximum weight on the unique tree path between its endpoints. This yields a partial order: every tree edge t must come before every non-tree edge e whose path includes t. The problem can be solved by counting linear extensions of this poset and summing the weights on tree edges accordingly.

A direct DP over all M edges is infeasible (M can be up to 190). A tractable approach uses the fact that the non-tree edges depend only on which tree edges have already appeared. We can group non-tree edges by the set of tree edges on their path (represented as a bitmask mask_e over A = N-1 tree edges). Let cnt[mask] be the number of non-tree edges whose path-set equals mask. For any subset S of tree edges that have already appeared, the number of non-tree edges whose prerequisites are all satisfied (i.e., all edges on their path are in S) is released(S) = sum_{mask ⊆ S} cnt[mask]. When building the final permutation from smallest to largest weight, we keep track of:

- S: which tree edges have appeared so far (a bitmask of A bits)
- j: how many non-tree edges have appeared so far (0..B where B = M - A)

From state (S, j), the next event can be:
- place another tree edge not in S (any of the A - |S| remaining tree edges) -> transitions to (S ∪ {bit}, j)
- place a non-tree edge from those already released but not yet placed -> there are released(S) - j such choices -> transitions to (S, j+1)

This yields a DP over states (S, j) with transitions:
- dp[S ∪ {b}][j] += dp[S][j] for each b not in S
