Plan:
- Build pos_q[v] = position of value v in q.
- Build a persistent segment tree over range [1..n] for pos_q. Create versions root[t] for t = 0..n where root[t] contains counts of all elements v with pos_p[v] ≤ t. We do this by iterating t from 1..n and inserting the element with p[t] at position pos_q[p[t]] into the previous version.
- For a query, after computing l1, r1, l2, r2 using the dynamic x, the answer is:
  ans = count(pos_p ≤ r1 and pos_q ∈ [l2, r2]) - count(pos_p ≤ l1-1 and pos_q ∈ [l2, r2])
  Using the persistent tree, those counts are:
  countR = query(root[r1], 1, n, l2, r2)
  countL = query(root[l1-1], 1, n, l2, r2)
  ans = countR - countL
- We update x for each query as x = 0 for the first query, else x = previous_answer + 1, and compute l1, r1, l2, r2 via f(z) = ((z - 1 + x) mod n) + 1 for each input z ∈ {a,b,c,d}.

This yields O((n + m) log n) time and O(n log n) memory, suitable for the given constraints. The persistent tree uses about n log n nodes (roughly 20 million for n=1e6), which fits within the memory limit. All computations use 32-bit ints where safe (counts up to n), and 64-bit for intermediate query state as needed.

