- b_i equals the count of onvac nodes in subtree(i) minus onvac[i]. So b_i = sum_subtree_onvac(i) - onvac[i].
- A naïve approach recomputes everything per day; would be O(n log n) per day, which is too slow for n, m up to 1e5.
- A typical efficient solution uses heavy-light decomposition (HLD) or Euler tour plus a data structure that supports: (a) range-add along the path root->x when x goes on vacation/returns (since all ancestors’ b_i change), and (b) quickly counting how many nodes i with onvac[i] == 0 have b_i > t_i. This is non-trivial because counting across all nodes with range-add updates requires maintaining a distribution of values (b_i) per node constrained by onvac[i].
