  "analysis": "We need to compute the shortest path from vertex 1 to vertex N in a graph built by M operations. Each operation adds a clique on the interval [L_i, R_i] with edge weight C_i between every pair within that interval. A direct naive construction would blow up since an interval could be as large as N and M up to 1e5. To solve efficiently, we model the interval-clique additions with auxiliary nodes and two segment trees, enabling O((N+M) log N) edges and a Dijkstra run over roughly O(N log N + M log N) edges.

Construction idea (graph model):
- We keep the N real vertex nodes (1..N).
- Build a segment-out tree (segOut) over leaves 1..N. Its internal nodes (size-1 of them) get new graph IDs. Leaves are vertices themselves.
  - From every vertex v, we connect v to all internal segOut nodes on the path from leaf v to the root (0-cost edges). This ensures v can reach any internal node that covers v.
  - For every operation i with interval [L_i, R_i], we decompose the interval into O(log N) segOut nodes or leaves. For each such node, add an edge from that node to the operation node I_i with cost 0. If a decomposition piece is a leaf (i.e., a single vertex s), we instead connect that vertex s to I_i with cost 0. This models v -> I_i with 0 cost for any v in [L_i, R_i].
- Build a segment-in tree (segIn) over leaves 1..N. Internal nodes have graph IDs. Leaves again are the vertex nodes.
  - We connect internal segIn nodes to their children with 0-cost edges to propagate down to leaves. If a child is a leaf, we connect the segIn internal node to that leaf (vertex) with 0 cost.
  - For each operation i, decompose [L_i, R_i] using segIn. For each piece (internal node or leaf), add an edge from I_i to that piece with cost C_i. This ensures I_i can reach any t in [L_i, R_i] with cost C_i.
- For each operation i we create an auxiliary node I_i (one per operation).

With this model, any pair (s, t) in an interval i has a path s -> I_i -> t with total cost C_i, which matches the clique addition. The graph is directed, but the construction yields symmetric reachability because for any s in [L_i, R_i] there is a path to I_i with 0, and from I_i to t with C_i; similarly, t can reach I_i with 0 and I_i to s with C_i. All edge weights are non-negative, so Dijkstra is correct.

Complexity considerations: number of vertices is roughly N + (size-1) + (size-1) + M, where size is the next power of two >= N. The total edges are O(N log N + M log N) due to the segOut and segIn connections plus the downward segIn edges. This fits comfortably within constraints for N, M up to 1e5 in optimized C++.

